WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.910
- Thank you all for
joining us on this webinar.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.550
For those who don't know me,
my name is Florian Carle.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.040
I'm the manager of the
Yale Quantum Institutes,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.190
and I'm the host of this
series of non-technical talks,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.110
co-sponsored by The Franke Program

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.690
in the Science and the Humanities.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:26.190
The idea of this series
was to talk about science,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.570
not in a scary way,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.620
but by mixing other
topics like humanities,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.330
art, design, music,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.870
and try to really engage on these topics

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.400
that are seen as difficult,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.090
but that we can really make
accessible to everybody.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.270
So this is the 15th part in this series,

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.770
and we are delighted to
have Klem Jankiewicz,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.880
who is a designer and uses
her practice to design

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.840
interface without no-code
for quantum computers.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.230
So it's very exciting to
have her this morning,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.573
or this afternoon currently in Israel.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.220
So Klem Jankiewicz design
and builds this interface

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.460
but before this, she was
working with museum, theater

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.510
and other educational
institution to create exhibition

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.210
and interactive installation,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.910
including one on the facade of the POLIN,

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.613
one of the most famous museum in Poland.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.370
And recently she has moved a little bit

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.370
on the field of quantum physics,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.370
working in collaboration with
physicists and engineers.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.030
She has worked with

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.790
the Centre of Quantum
Technology in Singapore,

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:44.580
Xanadu in Canada, Pasqal in France,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.327
and currently with Classiq
Technology in Israel.

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:52.140
And so the idea for her was
to build all this interface

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.130
and of course she will talk
about this in a few minutes,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.300
but she has developed

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.900
among other some game and
educational experience

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.960
like Quantum Flytrap, Pulser Studio,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.090
and Virtual Quantum Lab.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.837
And so we have a lot to talk about today,

00:02:09.837 --> 00:02:13.770
and so we are very excited
to have you at YQI virtually,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.100
but hopefully at some point on site.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.750
Thank you so much for joining us.

00:02:21.872 --> 00:02:24.000
- Hi, thank you for having me, Florian.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.173
It's, I'm really happy
to be here virtually.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.760
And yeah, and

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:37.200
I'll talk today about
mostly the project I did

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.670
in the quantum industry

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:45.120
and how I look at it and
what's important for me there.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.370
My name is Klem and I'm a designer

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.470
and for the last few years,

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.030
as Florian just mentioned,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.690
I've been working with
the quantum industry,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.320
mainly building visual
graphical user interfaces

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.776
to interact with the quantum technologies.

00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:05.703
And this is what I'll
be talking about today,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.870
and I'll start a bit about
user interfaces in general

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.410
and how I see this and
what's important for me,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.390
and why I think building

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:18.123
those visual interfaces brings value.

00:03:19.306 --> 00:03:21.930
And then I'll show you a
few examples of my works,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.860
so one will be Pulser
Studio we built for Pasqal.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.413
I'll show you Quantum Flytrap lab,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.850
an interactive optical table

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.560
which is an educational tool

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.380
and also show you a sneak
peek of something that we are

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.990
working on with Classiq
which is not yet released,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.530
so it'll be only show a
bit of it, only the design,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.400
but hopefully it'll be
available quite soon.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.460
And also I'll show one
non-quantum project,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.920
but also it's something that I think adds

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.753
to this

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.803
conversation about user interfaces.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.923
Yeah, so user interface,
what is a user interface?

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:09.111
A space where interaction between
humans and machines occur.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.730
So it's a way we interact
with the technology, right?

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.960
So a language, different interfaces are,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.960
we can think about it
as different languages

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.740
have to interact and
participate in the technology.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.300
And you know, of course there are

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.000
many, many different interfaces

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.370
starting with what you see on your screen,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.200
that's like the most obvious one.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.860
But also, of course,
like a mouse or keyboard,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.963
there are voice interfaces,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.620
there are interfaces based on code

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.810
or more visual ones

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.610
like the ones I'm designing

00:04:50.599 --> 00:04:51.990
and building.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.580
And we need all those
different kinds of interfaces

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.340
because they answer to
different types of users.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.340
They, and different use
cases, different situations.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:10.820
So for example, fully visual interfaced

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.680
probably could be a bit problematic

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.363
when the vision is impaired.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.110
So, anything from blindness

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.040
to looking at your phone in the full sun

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.840
when you can't really see anything, right?

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.060
So like different types of users,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.003
but also just different
situation, different use case.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:36.290
And so those different
types of interfaces, then,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.580
they answer to those different needs

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.390
and essentially

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.610
by choosing what type
of interface we build

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.650
for a specific technology

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:50.100
and how we design this
interface is a choice,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.970
is a decision of who can
participate in the technology.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.180
And this is something that
is very important for me,

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.480
especially if we want any,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.740
it's not only quantum,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.390
any technology to be inclusive.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.363
I think it's something
to really think about.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.593
And when we do talk
about quantum computing,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.640
the most popular types of
interfaces are very text-based.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.770
This is, so it's usually either code,

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.290
usually some kind of Python library

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.683
or of course the scientific texts,

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.190
and you know, these are great interfaces,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.760
maybe scientific text aren't
a typical kind of interface.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.680
But in this situation, it's,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.740
I do see it as a kind of interface

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.573
because this is,

00:06:41.573 --> 00:06:45.210
it is a way to understand the technology

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:48.033
or to participate in the technology.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.210
So these are of course great, right?

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:55.860
So this particular example is from Pasqal

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.220
because in a few slides I'll show

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.990
an example of a project,

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.333
of a different interface we did for them.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.200
So these are, you know,
really, really great,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.600
but they're not for everyone.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.940
So one obvious example would be

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.560
if I don't know Python,

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.160
I cannot really use a Python interface.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:19.413
And the same with scientific texts,

00:07:20.578 --> 00:07:24.854
they require quite a
substantial scientific knowledge

00:07:24.854 --> 00:07:26.643
to understand them.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.830
And when we talk about quantum computing,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.540
a technology that, you know, can

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.100
potentially really at certain point

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.820
can impact everyone's life,

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:42.000
can change a number of industries,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.350
then I think there are a
number of different use cases

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:50.940
and user groups that
these specific interfaces

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.570
don't necessarily, aren't
necessarily enough.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:57.850
And one example will be a public debate

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:02.490
that will be important
or is already important

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:06.240
and we should have ways and tools

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.350
for a more general public
to be able to participate

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.790
in this technology.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.490
Of course education,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.370
this is I think quite obvious

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.490
we should lower the entry point.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.180
We should make it much easier

00:08:23.497 --> 00:08:27.333
for people to enter this domain,

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.830
but also all sorts of business situation.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.513
And this is something that comes,

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.050
that I hear a lot with,

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:39.840
while working with the quantum industry,

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.710
that, you know, this is a
very, very complex technology

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:47.730
and sometimes there are certain situations

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:51.180
where maybe those non-physicists

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.540
or people who are working
with those technologies,

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.220
they do, they would require
some more intuitive way

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.150
to participate or understand or interact

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:04.323
with the technology.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.260
But I also think there are many ways

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.120
that it can be valuable for research

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.930
and even for the most advanced users.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.060
And I will also talk about it

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.323
with the specific examples
of the interfaces I did.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.880
Yeah, so I think it's
really, really important

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:31.380
to try to think of the different ways

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.800
that we can produce different interfaces,

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.260
different tools we can build

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:41.460
for people to access this technology

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.133
and to interact with this technology.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.660
And the specific type of interfaces

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.260
that I build are the visual interfaces,

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.270
the graphical user interfaces

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.480
or the no-code or low-code interfaces.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.750
And this is what I'll show you now,

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.453
a few examples of
projects I have worked on.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.840
So the first example I'll show is a

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:13.817
interface we built for Pasqal.

00:10:15.433 --> 00:10:18.840
So Pasqal is a startup from Paris.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.120
It was co-founded by Alain Aspect,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:25.353
the Nobel Prize winner from 2022.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.280
And we built for them this.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.400
It's like a visual and
interactive representation

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.720
of their technology

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.360
and it's a no-code development platform

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.373
for neutral atom quantum computing.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.820
And I'll go back to this slide

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:42.730
I just showed

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:45.603
a minute ago.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:47.483
And,

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.900
so these are the main ways

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.710
to interact with their technology,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.150
to understand the technology.

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.970
So, they have Pulser, a Python library,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:03.570
and of course a lot of amazing research,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.970
a lot of scientific papers.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.370
And again, these are great

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:11.640
but they were looking for
another way to showcase

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.220
their technology and another way to,

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.960
they were looking to build another way

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.962
for the users to be able to interact

00:11:18.962 --> 00:11:21.213
with their technology.

00:11:22.207 --> 00:11:23.040
And this is when

00:11:25.665 --> 00:11:28.680
we built the Pulser studio,

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.110
this no-code development platform,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:35.070
online platform for Pasqal.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.260
And I see those three types of,

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.380
those three interfaces as
just like different languages

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.380
we can use to interact
with the same technology,

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.190
three different representation
of the same technology.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:48.810
And they're all very useful

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:51.000
just in very different use cases

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.360
and for different users
for different situations.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:58.350
Okay, so this is Pulser studio.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.673
It's a beta version,

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:05.340
so it's available online.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.830
Anyone can enter it

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:10.383
at pulserstudio.pasqal.cloud.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:16.790
And it's a visual representation
of their technology,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.100
an interactive representation
of their technology.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.220
So the idea here was to go very low level

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.230
because it was, they
really wanted it to be

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:29.554
very specific for their
particular technology

00:12:29.554 --> 00:12:31.050
and their research.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:31.923
So,

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.790
yeah, and this is the interface.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:37.890
So what happens here?

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.560
First of all, we have the register,

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.383
the, an arrangement of atoms,

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:49.580
and then when we arrange
our atoms in certain way,

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:56.013
we can, we build sequences
of laser pulses that are,

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:05.610
that we send to specific,

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.893
to all of the atoms or specific atoms.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.970
And it's all very, very interactive.

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:12.803
It

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:15.870
can move everything around,

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:20.130
we can generate different patterns.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.440
We want it to be very, very intuitive

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.390
and something that without any knowledge

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.010
on building the algorithms,

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.130
people can use as well.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.930
We have the code panel here,

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.760
here as well also to show what's happening

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.190
and yeah, and these are

00:13:38.446 --> 00:13:39.279
the light pulses.

00:13:39.279 --> 00:13:44.279
We can easily change
everything, add pulses.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:46.650
And there's also a simulation.

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:48.393
So if,

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:54.000
oh, it's, but if it's a
smaller number of atoms,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:58.983
there is a simulation,
online simulation available.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:04.503
And we can see step by step
what is happening here,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.710
which laser

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.740
is being active when,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:17.400
so it's, yeah, it's all
very, very interactive

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.460
and intuitive and at the same style,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.000
and we have different features here.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:27.543
And at the same time we can
see what happens in the code.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.050
We can also, by highlighting
certain elements,

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:34.830
we can see how they are
described in Pulser.

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.470
Most important idea we had in mind

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.150
when designing this interface was

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.910
to make it really, really intuitive

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:45.090
because the technology is very complex.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.240
And then we wanted the
interface to not to add

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.270
any complexity to this interaction,

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:56.270
to leave all the complexity
to the actual technology.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:59.820
And we, what I really wanted it to work

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:02.770
in a way that even if I
don't know how to design

00:15:04.995 --> 00:15:07.140
a quantum algorithm that makes sense,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.000
like what you now see on the screen.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.530
Obviously this doesn't make sense

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.750
from the quantum perspective,

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:14.643
but you can still use the,

00:15:14.643 --> 00:15:17.160
you can still use the interface,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.660
you can still easily play with it.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.940
You can still arrange the atoms,

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.723
you can still make those
channels with laser pulses.

00:15:26.461 --> 00:15:30.270
We really, really wanted it
to be super, super intuitive.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.840
And when Pasqal published a beta version,

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.760
there was someone who wrote a review

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.040
and have a screenshot here

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.863
because I really liked
the way he described it.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.897
And he wrote that,

00:15:43.897 --> 00:15:47.250
"Pulser Studio is a fun
for the whole family."

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:51.810
And the way I understood it is

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:55.170
that it's something that can
be used by the professionals,

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.600
by quantum algorithm designer,

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.150
but at the same time it's
something that this person can

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:01.890
give to their kid and the kid can play

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:03.930
and just make, I don't know,

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:05.520
a smiley out of the atoms

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.610
and just, you know, play
around with the light pulses

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:10.650
and arrange them in different way.

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:11.483
And

00:16:13.272 --> 00:16:14.520
it's still playful and fun.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.860
And for me it really captures the idea

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:20.643
what we really wanted
to do with this project.

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.513
And one way of working,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.670
one thing we did in the process
of designing the interface

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.310
to make it very intuitive was

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:39.780
to think about what is familiar for,

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.060
what would be familiar for the users?

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.070
And of course if we're talking about

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.300
visual graphical interfaces for

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.500
neutral atom quantum computing,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.620
there is no, there are no other tools.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:53.580
So it's not like, oh yeah,

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.440
there are those 20 other tools

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.590
and now we can only think about
how to make it better there.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:00.690
We didn't have a roadmap here,

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:02.610
so we are looking at other solutions

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:06.930
from different industries
and different areas.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.193
And for example,

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:14.160
for the channels with laser pulses,

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.010
we looked at all sorts of software

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:20.670
for editing music and making music.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:24.900
So, mostly editing music or editing sound.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:28.770
And of course the use case is
very, very different, right?

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.440
Because you know, here
it's we're editing sound

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.620
and here we're designing
a quantum algorithm.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:35.910
But in the end of the day,

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:40.560
these are some channels
with a certain timeline

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:44.100
and with those waveforms that
the users can move around,

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.470
can change, can edit.

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.510
So we, to make it intuitive,

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:50.520
we tried to learn as much as possible

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:53.457
from those different kind of softwares,

00:17:53.457 --> 00:17:55.740
but that are so well researched

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:59.313
and there are so many examples
what works best there.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:03.790
And so this was one of
the things we tried to do

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:09.120
in this project and also the simulation.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:12.990
So with those, the smaller setups,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.450
it's an automatic
simulation up to six atoms,

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:20.450
and it's possible also to
simulate setups up to 12 atoms.

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:24.570
And there was,

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:28.830
when we were user testing the solution,

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.540
one of the physicists,

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.270
one of the develop,

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.820
the people who develop
Pasqal's technology,

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.883
so one of the people who
know about it the most,

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:41.077
this person told us that,

00:18:41.077 --> 00:18:44.340
"I don't even have to think
what happens physics-wise,

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:46.590
I can just follow what's on the screen."

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:48.490
And I really liked it because

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:51.780
it was quite obvious for us,

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:55.230
this is, that this will be
good for the business cases,

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:57.660
it'll be great for education.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:00.600
But then I was really happy
that it was also valuable

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:04.530
for their own internal research team,

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:08.730
so the people that know
the most in the world

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:12.360
about Pasqal technology.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:13.830
And I know that they still use it

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:14.663
and that

00:19:16.491 --> 00:19:19.230
a lot of the physicists and
engineers in the Pasqal's team,

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:21.153
they still use it.

00:19:23.545 --> 00:19:26.550
And

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:29.680
this is also like a
very, very small example

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:35.670
of a very basic example
of how it can be useful

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:40.170
in this R and D, like
for an R and D use case.

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:43.713
So, a research use case
or development use case.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:45.443
And

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:53.130
you can see on the left side the register,

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.220
the six atoms, six qubits,

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.430
and on the left side you see the code.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:02.250
And when we highlight the atoms,

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:04.653
we can see where they
actually are in the code.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:07.650
And when you look at it,

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:09.540
it's quite obvious that, you know,

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.330
to arrange the atoms in this way,

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:14.310
it's very intuitive,

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.530
it's very simple to do it

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:19.203
in this very graphical, visual way,

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:23.040
but to do it in code, it's
just not the right tool.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.600
It sounds,

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:26.913
it's something that's quite obvious.

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:31.080
And of course there
are many different ways

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:32.550
the researchers use it,

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:36.990
but the, I think this specific one shows

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:39.660
in this minimum like very small example of

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:44.493
how it is useful for the researchers.

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.410
And of course, you
know, from the beginning

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:52.860
when we are developing this solution,

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:55.260
this business case was
very important for us.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:58.610
So the, all the business
situations and this,

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:01.470
it just so much more efficient,

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:06.470
so much, it's much easier
to showcase the technology

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:11.520
when, the business people on Pasqal side,

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:12.900
but also the client side

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:14.970
when they have something
that is interactive,

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:18.600
that is visual, that
doesn't require, you know,

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:23.193
two months of studying
of how to use something.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.100
And so this was from the beginning

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.850
very, very important point for us,

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.110
how to show,

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:36.110
how to make this interface
that it's very intuitive,

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:39.120
it shows the technology instantly,

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.853
but at the time it's a real thing.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.200
For now, if you go to the website,

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:48.870
it's still not connected to Pasqal's QPUs,

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:51.420
but the moment they
release the QPUs to public,

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:52.551
it'll be connected.

00:21:52.551 --> 00:21:54.670
So anyone will be able

00:21:56.392 --> 00:22:00.780
to execute an algorithm
built in Pulser Studio

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.333
on their actual quantum hardware.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:08.673
And now I'll show you another project.

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:14.310
And so this is the Quantum
Flytrap virtual lab.

00:22:14.310 --> 00:22:17.403
It's an interactive,

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:23.240
also available online,
interactive optical table.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:27.600
So kind of a sandbox where
users can drag and drop

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:32.600
elements and make experiments
or recreate experiments.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:36.810
And this project started
at CQT in Singapore

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:38.403
over four years ago.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:40.920
And we went there,

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:42.540
were a group of people,

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:45.190
work with physicists
and software engineers

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:47.880
and we worked

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:52.880
on a game or a computer
game about quantum physics.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.560
And later we continued the project

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.780
and it became the Quantum Flytrap projects

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.970
and we decided to go
more into the direction

00:23:02.443 --> 00:23:06.450
of an educational tool rather than a game.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.840
It's free to use for anyone.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:14.280
And I know that a lot of
educators around the world are

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:19.280
using it from high school,
high school teachers,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:22.020
there's number of online courses

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:26.850
that use the tool during the courses,

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:28.410
but also for, at universities.

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:31.588
For example, the Oxford University,

00:23:31.588 --> 00:23:34.773
they're using it at the
quantum information course.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:37.440
Yeah so it's,

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:39.060
this one is mainly educational.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:42.393
This was always the main idea here.

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:47.013
And I'll show now the platform itself.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:55.170
So we have this board,

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:58.710
this is this virtual optical table

00:23:58.710 --> 00:23:59.543
where we can

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:03.010
drag and drop elements

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.910
and just start like here

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:11.410
and we can edit all of the elements

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:21.200
to create whatever
experiment we'd like to see.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:22.403
And

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:26.850
there is, we have up to,

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:29.010
we have a simulation.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:29.843
So it's

00:24:32.599 --> 00:24:37.599
the same simulation we used
for the project for Pasqal,

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:40.230
for Pulser Studio,

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:41.910
so it's not only, you know,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:43.950
a playful, colorful visualization

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:47.160
but it's also like all the math is there

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:51.420
so users can see what
happens step by step.

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:55.140
And we have different
features that users can use

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:59.310
to learn more about what's
happening on the screen.

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:02.440
And we have up to four entangled photons

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.807
and we have a number of
saved experiments for,

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:15.180
because again, this is
an educational tool,

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:19.780
so this is, people can look at
different, famous experiments

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:24.667
and see how to recreate them in our lab.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:30.093
Go back to the presentation now.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:37.530
And the idea and the inspiration
is quite obvious here,

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:40.410
of course, it's a visual,

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:43.200
it's an interactive online representation

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.900
and simulation of optical table.

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:47.970
And of course we looked a lot about,

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:51.900
on how these experiments are represented

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:56.100
in the scientific literature.

00:25:56.100 --> 00:26:00.150
So this is of course very,
in a way very similar.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:04.300
We just made it interactive

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:07.650
and maybe also a bit more playful.

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:09.780
And the project, as I
said at the beginning,

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.740
the project started as a game

00:26:13.184 --> 00:26:17.390
and later evolved into this
more educational experience.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:20.400
And at certain point we are thinking

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:22.860
about changing the interface,

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:27.570
this graphical layer to
something a bit more serious.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.540
But we talked with the
users and we found out

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:31.373
that actually

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.590
majority of people like
the fact that it was

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.210
all colorful and playful,

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:37.590
maybe because it's educational,

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:42.270
so it's probably students and
young people that like games.

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:46.860
So we decided to stay
with this game-y feeling,

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:47.763
game-y look.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:51.330
And one thing I really liked,

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:54.600
and I think it was very
valuable from the beginning

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:57.700
with this project was
that we were developing it

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:01.770
very closely with our users.

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:03.480
So on the one hand,

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.190
because it's free to use for everyone,

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.090
so there were just a lot of people

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:10.290
sending us messages, emails,

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:11.520
we have a Discord channel,

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:14.280
so talking to us and
telling us their ideas

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.680
and what they would like to see there.

00:27:16.680 --> 00:27:17.970
Also, what doesn't work,

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:21.813
if we had some bugs, that
was also very, very useful.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:25.920
But we were also asking other researchers

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:29.580
and scientists, physicists to tell us

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.170
what they would like to see there,

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:34.230
what do they think would be great to have,

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:36.150
what features, what elements,

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:39.780
what kind of experiments
they would like to see.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:41.493
And for example,

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:44.590
someone told us that it would be great

00:27:46.926 --> 00:27:50.190
to see the Elitzur-Vaidman bomb test,

00:27:50.190 --> 00:27:52.140
the thought experiment.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:54.660
So we were like, okay, great, we'll,

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:55.860
we had a bomb already,

00:27:55.860 --> 00:28:00.483
so we had to do some changes
to be able to add this,

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:03.963
to add this experiment,

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:05.760
but we did it.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:09.780
And then my colleague actually talked with

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:13.890
Lev Vaidman and he showed
him the experiment.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:16.380
And I don't think he was,

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:18.240
he had a lot of feedback.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:21.900
He didn't immediately
understand everything

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:22.950
that was happening there,

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:26.460
so it was obviously very, very
important feedback for us.

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:29.106
And we tried to incorporate as much

00:28:29.106 --> 00:28:31.383
of his feedback as possible,

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.920
but we also asked him
about some other things

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:35.940
he would like to see there

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:38.790
and what could be simulated there.

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:40.117
And for example, he mentioned,

00:28:40.117 --> 00:28:41.620
"Oh, it would be great to have

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:46.260
indistinguishable particles
for two-photon interference

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.660
or the GHZ entangled state."

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.027
So we were like, "Okay,
great, let's do it."

00:28:51.932 --> 00:28:55.410
And we added these and this
was from the beginning,

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.480
this was the process,
we were asking people,

00:28:57.480 --> 00:28:59.040
and of course, you know, not,

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:02.220
we weren't incorporating
every single feedback,

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:05.130
but from the beginning this
process was very, very natural.

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:08.400
And we were just okay, trying to listen

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:12.507
and like, "Okay, let's do
it, this sounds great."

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.230
And you know, as I said,

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:19.170
of course the main usage was educational

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:24.170
and a lot of educators used
and still use this tool.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.900
And we had a really nice situation

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:32.900
that I will talk about now
with a much older version.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.250
It was few years ago, so
it was much, much older,

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:38.760
much simpler version of the lab.

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.610
It was just the beginning of the pandemic

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:46.203
and a professor from
Stanford reached out to us.

00:29:50.102 --> 00:29:50.935
She found the tool online

00:29:50.935 --> 00:29:52.440
and she reached out and she said

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:57.270
that she usually has those
hands-on, experimental classes,

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:59.220
but now the pandemic started

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:03.000
and everything moved online

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.090
and suddenly she was left with a textbook.

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:09.630
So she was looking for something
a bit more interactive,

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.540
a bit more fun for the student,

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:14.428
and she found our project.

00:30:14.428 --> 00:30:16.410
And then of course she and the students,

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:19.020
they gave us a lot of feedback
and this was really great

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:24.020
and we made a lot of changes
according to their feedback.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:25.957
And at the end she told us that,

00:30:25.957 --> 00:30:28.110
"Even when it becomes possible to teach

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:29.430
my class in person again,

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:31.860
your website, your project
could help me scale

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:35.070
to a larger class size than is possible

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:38.457
when I require equipment for
hands-on demonstrations."

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:42.720
And I really, really liked what she said

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:45.530
because this is, in a way,

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:49.410
it really talks about what
I said in the beginning.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:51.300
So of course this interface,

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:55.290
this is not instead of
the actual lab, right?

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:59.880
But it's a different
way to interact with it

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:02.460
and it's a different
way to learn about it.

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:05.820
So this professor, she would
still have this experimental,

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:07.770
this hands-on class,

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:09.870
but she could use this tool

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:14.853
to make a class to a much
larger group of students.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:18.390
And for me, this is the
main reason to build

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:20.640
those visual interfaces,

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:23.760
not to exchange a
different type of interface

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:28.140
but to open this technology
to some new situations,

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:30.213
new use cases and new users.

00:31:32.295 --> 00:31:35.700
And now I will show a third project,

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.290
a third quantum project.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:38.610
And this is, it'll,

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:39.720
it's just a sneak peek.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:42.660
I can't show the full project yet.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:45.033
It's not released, we're
still working on it.

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:48.483
So I'll only show a bit of the design.

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:53.736
And this is something we
are working on with Classiq.

00:31:53.736 --> 00:31:57.960
Classiq is an Israeli
startup based in Tel Aviv.

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.600
And we are working on this kind of

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:02.490
visual graphical interface,

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:06.933
graphical user interface
for the Classiq platform.

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.883
And just to give you a bit of context,

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:17.940
so this will be part of
a much broader product.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:19.740
So it's not,

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:20.973
it's only a part of the,

00:32:21.993 --> 00:32:23.970
of our Classiq product.

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:25.540
And the Classiq platform

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.823
allows user to design,

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:33.900
to build, to design a model

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:37.770
that can be later synthesized
into a quantum circuit

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:42.030
that is optimized in certain
way depending on the use case,

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:43.380
depending on the needs.

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:46.440
And later users can execute

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:51.060
the circuit on any of the
available quantum backends,

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:55.050
so quantum simulators and
actual quantum hardware

00:32:55.050 --> 00:32:57.240
on in, all in one platform.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:59.610
And the project I'm showing you will be

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:03.150
part of this first step,

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:05.460
so building the model,

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.493
building the functional
model, the algorithm.

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:12.060
And as I showed you this slide,

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:15.520
it's, we are building this
kind of node interface

00:33:19.671 --> 00:33:21.780
and this again comes to this question

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:26.130
about how to make it as
intuitive as possible

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:29.820
and how can we learn from other solutions

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:31.170
from different industries,

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.210
different use cases,

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:34.620
but how can we learn from them,

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:37.470
from things that are
researched really well?

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:40.290
There are a lot of different products

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.090
and how can we learn from there

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:45.360
and build something for quantum?

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:48.610
And we decided to use this
kind of node interface

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.783
and on the one hand,

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:56.760
the things we researched and we looked at,

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:58.500
so one hand it will,

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:01.260
this whole world of electronic circuits,

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:02.880
schematics, netlists,

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:05.610
it's maybe not the most user friendly,

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:08.250
but there's a lot of
important solutions there

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:09.570
that are very, very relevant

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:12.033
for building quantum algorithms.

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:17.790
And of course, more
typical node interfaces,

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:21.930
for example, for 3D modeling like Blender,

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:23.820
there's some music software,

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:26.280
there's a lot of automation software.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.750
So there are quite a lot
of different solutions

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.420
that were developed for
very different industries

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:34.890
and we're trying to learn from them

00:34:34.890 --> 00:34:38.073
and see how can we translate it for,

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:40.053
into quantum.

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:43.803
And it's,

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:48.000
we try, I hope it will be very simple,

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:52.740
so the users will just choose blocks,

00:34:52.740 --> 00:34:55.180
either functions or gates

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:58.410
or they can create their own blocks

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:01.390
and they just wire them together

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:07.110
and they can add more and
more blocks and wire them

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:10.773
and build the algorithm in
this very, very visual way.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:16.740
They can merge them if you,

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:18.270
they have a lot of small blocks,

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:19.800
they can merge them into bigger blocks,

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:24.780
they can build their
own blocks from scratch.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:25.613
And

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:30.150
I think it'll be also really good

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:32.850
for adding external data.

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:34.770
This is also something that comes back

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:36.120
when I'm talking with users

00:35:36.120 --> 00:35:39.660
and especially users
from different domains.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.400
So, someone from finance,

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.383
maybe they would just like to have this,

00:35:43.383 --> 00:35:46.650
some kind of finance block
where they only upload

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:50.670
their CSV or some other
format of the data.

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:53.400
Also really great for all
sorts of visualizations.

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:55.623
That's very natural language here.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:59.193
And of course we're still,

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:03.540
it's still not released,

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:05.580
so the use cases,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:06.780
it's only something that

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:10.334
we think of

00:36:10.334 --> 00:36:14.610
and I hope to learn
about different use cases

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:16.530
when we do release it.

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:18.810
But some of the situations where I think

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:23.400
it could be useful would
be like, for example,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.770
for maybe domain experts,

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:27.550
like I said

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.840
about finance

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:34.620
and people that they might not,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.230
maybe they don't want
to work in code so much.

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:38.700
It might be easier to,

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:41.760
for them to work with something visual.

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:44.670
But also I hope it will
be useful for researchers,

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:46.470
so I can imagine the situation

00:36:46.470 --> 00:36:49.620
that someone is working on the research,

00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:51.480
they're publishing the research

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:53.192
and then they can have this

00:36:53.192 --> 00:36:56.850
interactive

00:36:56.850 --> 00:36:59.700
visualization of their research

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:01.920
or interactive representation
of their research

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:06.087
that anyone can play
with, can interact with

00:37:06.087 --> 00:37:09.990
and can maybe build upon
in a very intuitive way.

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:11.850
And of course for learning,

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:14.640
again all the visual solutions,

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:17.460
I think this is something

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:19.923
that is really a no-brainer for learning.

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:24.510
And I can imagine someone opening

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:26.970
an example of an algorithm

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:28.560
and then looking inside

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:30.780
and seeing what are the
different building blocks

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:32.760
of this algorithm

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:36.090
and trying to build their
own way, their own algorithm,

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:41.090
so I think this for sure will
be really valuable for them.

00:37:42.664 --> 00:37:44.010
And again, this is just a sneak peek.

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:46.020
This is just a design.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:48.330
So, but I hope that it'll be,

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.460
some first version will be available soon.

00:37:50.460 --> 00:37:53.399
So stay, you, yeah,

00:37:53.399 --> 00:37:55.870
you can check the platform

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.043
to see.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:03.543
Hopefully soon it will be released.

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.140
And the last project I
wanted to show you is

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:09.640
something very, very different

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:12.233
and

00:38:15.291 --> 00:38:17.070
it in a way comes back to this idea

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:20.640
of different users and different situation

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:22.350
and producing very different

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:27.350
or researching very different
types of interfaces,

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:31.143
of ways we can interact with technology.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.620
And this is a project
I did a few years ago.

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:38.370
It was an art installation
for a theater play

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:43.203
about the human-technology interaction.

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:46.920
And it built this,

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:51.810
I designed this swarm of

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:53.010
electronic objects.

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:55.540
I designed objects and the electronic part

00:38:56.668 --> 00:38:59.070
and those objects were placed

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:00.370
around the actors

00:39:01.616 --> 00:39:04.983
and were also some kind
of an actor, in a way.

00:39:06.390 --> 00:39:08.640
And the interaction was happening,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:11.220
so the kind of the interface was,

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:12.690
it was a voice interface

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:16.770
so the actors would interact
with this installation,

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:18.630
with their voice

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:23.370
and the installation
would reply with voice

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:26.160
and with sound and with light

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:28.320
and would change depending on

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.393
how the actors interacted with it.

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:37.530
And this is, you know,
when think about this,

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:39.090
it's a very different use case

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:42.780
and a very different user type

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:45.513
than than physicist, for example.

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:49.620
But when we think actors on the stage,

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:52.890
of course having an
interface where they click

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:55.290
somewhere on the screen with a mouse,

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:56.940
that wouldn't be suitable.

00:39:56.940 --> 00:40:00.030
That would probably be very boring

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:03.123
and just not something they needed.

00:40:04.298 --> 00:40:06.060
And of course this is
in a way exaggerated,

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:09.570
but I wanted to show it that it,

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:12.010
we should really think about interfaces

00:40:13.200 --> 00:40:17.010
as all the different ways we
can interact with technology

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:20.230
and that each different type of interface

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:25.260
refers to a different
or answers to the needs

00:40:25.260 --> 00:40:28.423
of a different group of users
or a different situation,

00:40:28.423 --> 00:40:30.150
a use case.

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:32.823
For example, physicist or actors.

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:36.510
Yeah, and these are the four projects

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:37.800
I wanted to show you today,

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:39.600
so thank you very much.

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:41.790
- Thank you so much for such a great talk.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:45.570
Thank you for sharing
with us all your projects.

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:47.910
The first thing that when
we see all of these projects

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:50.940
and especially interface,
we see a lot of purple.

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:52.380
And I have a question about this

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.720
because also at YQI,

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:56.580
some of the work that we are doing,

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:59.580
like most of the quantum
is becoming purple.

00:40:59.580 --> 00:41:01.590
Like, it's shifting towards this color.

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:03.240
I was wondering what's,

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:04.410
why did you pick this color?

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:07.110
Is it because it's more of a dark system

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:09.390
or like is it, did you
have a meaning behind it?

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:11.790
Like what's, why did you
pick this (indistinct)?

00:41:14.550 --> 00:41:15.510
- Yeah, that, you know, the,

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:17.940
it's funny 'cause when I
put all of them together,

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:20.193
I also thought that
it's very, very purple.

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:22.353
It was something that,

00:41:24.651 --> 00:41:26.940
it was very intentional
with Quantum Flytrap

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:30.030
and I really researched at the beginning

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:32.844
what are the colors that kind of.

00:41:32.844 --> 00:41:33.677
(Klem Jankiewicz coughs)

00:41:33.677 --> 00:41:36.960
Sorry, make people think
about quantum things.

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:38.550
And I was very happy

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:42.210
to find out that it was
all this like purple,

00:41:42.210 --> 00:41:44.640
sometimes like yellow, pink.

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:49.091
But I think it's probably
a mix of, you know,

00:41:49.091 --> 00:41:50.860
what works well

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:55.200
and what I personally like.

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:57.933
It's always somewhere in between.

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:04.250
So, and these are all like
dark background interfaces,

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:09.760
which I think this mainly
comes from the fact that

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:13.620
you know, with each project this would be

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:16.110
always a discussion
and we would think that

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:19.350
okay, with the users we have with mind,

00:42:19.350 --> 00:42:23.440
usually people tend to
choose the dark mode

00:42:24.300 --> 00:42:25.830
that's, but yeah, that's.

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:28.980
- Could be a debate in
the labs, the dark mode.

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:32.280
- Yeah, that there is a lot of definitely,

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:33.240
but that's why, you know,

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:36.510
it's like a first stage
and ideally there should be

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:38.940
the light mode added as well.

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:41.010
But also for sure something that's,

00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:44.100
I do like strong color.

00:42:44.100 --> 00:42:46.262
- Yeah, it's very.
- To be used.

00:42:46.262 --> 00:42:48.267
- Very beautiful, yeah.

00:42:48.267 --> 00:42:51.270
The reason why we are
using purple a lot is

00:42:51.270 --> 00:42:52.770
because on the (indistinct),

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:54.060
you have like the one and zero

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:55.050
and then like it's a mix of

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:57.390
usually physicists use red and blue,

00:42:57.390 --> 00:43:00.810
and so to sort of like hint
at the supervision of states,

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:03.750
it's nice to have this like
purple-ish thing that comes in.

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:05.820
So like it was interesting to see

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.640
even if it's not the main thing.

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:10.290
Another question that I have,

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:11.790
and for people in the audience,

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:14.760
if you are at YQI, feel
free to unmute the room

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:15.593
if you want to talk

00:43:15.593 --> 00:43:18.780
or you can use the Q&amp;A feature
at the bottom of the screen

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.223
to ask your questions.

00:43:21.150 --> 00:43:23.310
One question I have is that

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:26.010
how did you jump from doing installation

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:27.660
and educational installation to quantum?

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:30.330
Like, quantum is seen as often scary.

00:43:30.330 --> 00:43:32.250
Like, what was your process

00:43:32.250 --> 00:43:33.240
and like how much did you know

00:43:33.240 --> 00:43:36.480
before going into quantum
to working on this?

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:39.300
Because I think to create
very valuable interface,

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:40.590
you need to have a good understanding

00:43:40.590 --> 00:43:41.957
of what's happening on the,

00:43:41.957 --> 00:43:44.070
in the technology to be able to recreate

00:43:44.070 --> 00:43:48.180
and simplify it without
oversimplifying it to,

00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:49.173
for the audience.

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:54.420
- Yeah, so I started at CQT.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:56.680
This was like literally the first time

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:01.383
I started to learn anything
about quantum technologies.

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:07.170
And you know, it started a year earlier.

00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:12.170
I was participating in
this project made by,

00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:15.180
run by MIT Media Lab in Berlin.

00:44:15.180 --> 00:44:17.220
They had, did this like European

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:19.710
short, like one-week project

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:24.570
and invited all sorts of people
from Europe to participate

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:29.100
also about this crossroads
of technology and design,

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:30.033
music, art.

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:33.930
And I, you know, and
there was one person there

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:35.280
who was a quantum physicist,

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:37.110
and then somehow we stayed in touch

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:38.700
and a year later he was,

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:41.430
I saw that he's looking for people to come

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:45.270
with him to build a game
about quantum physics at CQT.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:47.580
And I thought, "Oh that's perfect.

00:44:47.580 --> 00:44:49.500
What could go wrong?"

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:52.170
So it literally started like this.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.690
This wasn't intentional
and I thought it'll be,

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:57.330
you know, a fun, weird
project I'll have in my CV.

00:44:57.330 --> 00:44:58.740
And I, and then, you know,

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.170
I started working on this
and I started working

00:45:01.170 --> 00:45:03.907
with a lot of physicists and I thought,

00:45:03.907 --> 00:45:05.079
"Wow,

00:45:05.079 --> 00:45:08.820
this is the most interesting
and most amazing thing

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:09.987
you can imagine."

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:13.380
And I thought, "Okay,
that's what I want to do.

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:17.040
I want to stay in this industry."

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:19.560
But of course all of these
projects, you know, it's,

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:21.540
these aren't only my projects.

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:24.390
There was always a team of
developers and physicists.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:29.390
So it's, I wouldn't be
able even to do the design.

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:32.400
It's a collaboration of the whole team

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:34.320
and it's everyone's ideas.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:38.883
So there was, you know, a lot
of physicists involved here.

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:42.630
And with the time I of
course know more and more,

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:44.313
but I'm still not a physicist.

00:45:45.450 --> 00:45:47.250
- No, but that's, I
think that's the beauty

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:50.940
of working at the interface
of science and technology

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:52.830
and art and humanities is that like

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:55.500
you really need to bring
people from multiple skills

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:58.020
because while a physicist
