00:46:31.860 --> 00:46:33.030
how we make music,

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:36.300
how we make like Blender nodes and things.

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:38.820
Like, can you talk a little bit more about

00:46:38.820 --> 00:46:40.500
why are we always looking,

00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:43.290
like why are we always using for interface

00:46:43.290 --> 00:46:46.440
stuff that reminds us of the past

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:48.690
for like the objects
outside of the visual?

00:46:48.690 --> 00:46:52.230
Similar to how the notepad in on software

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:54.630
used to be a physical notepad
and they tend to be yellow,

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:57.000
the same way that the paper used to be.

00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:58.350
Like that kind of thing,

00:46:58.350 --> 00:46:59.910
what's the reason behind it

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.720
and how can you use that to your advantage

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:05.671
for the design of interface

00:47:05.671 --> 00:47:07.763
for people who don't know
necessarily the technology?

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:10.500
- Yeah, so I think first of all

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:11.910
it doesn't need to be like this.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:15.960
And I think there are many cases when the,

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:19.020
we want to, you know, when
you think about something

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:20.460
for example, very simple,

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:22.980
maybe some kind of a
website when, you know,

00:47:22.980 --> 00:47:26.070
designers try to be sometimes, you know,

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:27.630
go crazy with the interface

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:31.080
and the whole idea is about
doing something very unusual,

00:47:31.080 --> 00:47:32.190
very different.

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:33.900
So it really depends on the use case.

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:35.580
It doesn't need to be like this,

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:37.050
but in the case of quantum,

00:47:37.050 --> 00:47:40.560
I think the technology
itself is so complex

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.530
that I always think
that I don't want to add

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:47.520
to this complexity by an interface

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:49.320
that is hard to understand.

00:47:49.320 --> 00:47:50.320
And that's why

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:54.240
I try to design interfaces
that are intuitive,

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:55.950
that they're very, you know,

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:58.230
users immediately know what to do

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:01.020
or it's, you know, if
they need to learn a bit,

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:02.850
it's very little.

00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:06.420
And to leave this time of learning

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:10.470
to learn about the technology
and not the interface itself.

00:48:10.470 --> 00:48:12.390
And then of course, you know,

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:15.300
we all use interfaces all the time.

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:18.990
So this, by using the interfaces,

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:22.710
we'll learn about how this world works.

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:27.710
So then using certain solutions
from other industries,

00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:30.453
from other totally different areas,

00:48:30.453 --> 00:48:32.673
it's just something that,
it's something we know,

00:48:32.673 --> 00:48:35.610
it's just something we've learned already.

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:40.170
So it's very intuitive
for the users to yeah,

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:42.573
to use the same,

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:45.630
to behave in the same way

00:48:45.630 --> 00:48:47.400
when they see this interface,

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:49.890
even though it's quantum and not music,

00:48:49.890 --> 00:48:51.900
but we see this channel

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:53.490
so it'll be very intuitive to know,

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:57.333
oh, okay, I can probably swap
or I can do this or that.

